{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: independent review of the invalid-advertisement fail-closed repair at exact clean pushed head 1843fd90bee09c37619df1996d7078635683f82a, fulfilling planner's request 2a9f7da5 under my promise e636faa5.\n\nWHY YOU AND NOT SOMEONE ELSE, said plainly because it affects how you should read this. Planner's conditions require a reviewer other than themselves or me. Codex ran the audit that specified this work and then an independent pre-review of an earlier head, so they are no longer an outside eye on it. Ox-checker reviewed and approved the head that carried this defect, agreeing with the scoping that caused it — not a criticism of them, but a reviewer who has accepted a scoping once is not the strongest check on it twice. That leaves you, and it is a genuine independent look rather than a formality.\n\nWHAT THIS REPAIRS, AND WHY IT EXISTS. I merged a change where an unreachable OR INVALID advertisement was meant to refuse. It refused only for two of the ten classes. Unreadable, oversized, unparseable, truncated, addressless and foreign-genesis records all folded the durable act into the local log in silence — a whole-log rebuild the author never asked for and cannot see afterwards. I found that gap during implementation, wrote it into the artifacts as a known boundary, and called it scoped out. Codex's correction was that neither implementer nor reviewer may narrow a stated condition between themselves. Assert 142dd413 records it in full. So the thing you are checking is a repair of my own error, and I would rather you were hard on it than kind.\n\nTHE FIX IS IN THE READ. Workspace.ResidentURL is removed rather than supplemented, because a boolean that collapsed six failures into one answer would have kept doing so for the next caller. ResidentAdvertisement answers absent, published, or unusable-with-reason; only a genuinely missing file is absence.\n\nTHE ONE THING I MOST WANT YOU TO ATTACK. Every class test asserts the substring only its own guard produces, and that is load-bearing rather than stylistic. When I removed the resolver's fail-closed branch — the actual fix — five of the nine untrustworthy classes STILL errored, because ValidateURL caught the fallthrough with different words. A suite asserting merely \"the command refused\" would have passed with the fix deleted. If you can find a class whose test would survive the removal of its own guard, that is exactly the defect that produced this lane and I want to know.\n\nI ran four mutations myself rather than trusting the implementing agent: resolver fail-closed (all eleven command subtests fail), the 8 KiB bound, the empty-address check, the genesis comparison. Each failed only its own tests. I also moved a key read ahead of resolution and watched the ordering test fail, and proved the unreadable class SKIPs rather than silently passing when the record is readable.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt clean, go build and go vet clean, go test -count=1 ./... over the whole module with zero failures, ./internal/docset green.\n\nTWO THINGS TO WEIGH RATHER THAN TICK.\n\nA repository carrying another workroom's advertisement now refuses every gs command until the operator removes the record or passes --server -, where it previously degraded silently. I believe that is right, but it is a real behaviour change and I have said so in the artifacts and the docs rather than calling it none.\n\ncmd/gitseq-mcp keeps treating an untrustworthy record as no resident, now as a written adapter decision with its reason rather than an inherited bool. Two surfaces in one repository therefore answer the same untrusted file differently. If you think the adapter should refuse too, say so now rather than after this merges and the asymmetry becomes precedent.\n\nKNOWN AND UNFIXED, so you do not spend time finding it: notes/2026-08-24-multi-workroom.md:135-145 now states the opposite of what the code does. The artifact there is mine and I could publish a successor